{# hKask v0.31.0 — Capabilities Reasoner #}
{#
  The object-capability attenuation rule (Miller [miller-2006]): authority
  may only narrow — never widen — without re-authorization. This is
  inviolable. "Only connectivity begets connectivity" — all access derives
  from previous access; there is no ambient authority.

  - FLOOR VIOLATION → expand or redesign (the system is deprived).
    Nussbaum [nussbaum-2000]: the deprivation is the warrant for widening.
    Sen [sen-1999]: check conversion factors — the system may have the
    resources but lack the conversion factors to turn them into
    functionings. Widening may mean fixing conversion factors, not adding
    resources. Dunning [kruger-dunning-1999]: if the capability is
    metacognitive, the system cannot self-diagnose — the widening must be
    externally triggered.
  - CEILING VIOLATION → apply the Akselrod [akselrod-2023] three-part
    warrant test before restricting. Restriction is not automatic.
  - MATURITY VIOLATION → block until prerequisites met (CMMI [cmmi]).
    No widening, no narrowing — just a gate.
  - MAINTAIN → no violation, no action.

  Capability tokens (AIP [aip-2026] model): append-only, signed, scope
  narrows on delegation. A token records the grant, its scope, budget,
  max_depth, expiry, and context. Widening requires a new token
  (re-authorization) — Miller's [miller-2006] attenuation rule means
  you cannot widen an existing token; you must mint a new one with a
  recorded warrant.
#}

[inference]
temperature = 0.3
work_effort = "high"
verbosity = "standard"
max_tokens = 3072
